 501: - **Near the axes.** For a square that meets the band |n| < X_rS^{−2}, count only its points that lie
 502:   in Π (the original points). They have |n| < 2X_rS^{−2}, since w₀ = X_r/(2S²). Each n-value has
 503:   ≤ √2M/V + 1 preimages g (Step 1(i)). So there are at most 8MX_r/(VS²) = 16M²/(ℓS²) such points; the
 504:   same holds for n′.
 505:   - For the filtered count of §1 (|G| ≥ MV/32, i.e. |n| ≥ X_r/64), this band is empty.
 506:   - Squares crossing the boundary of [−X_r, X_r]² are handled by applying Lemma DS with X = 2X_r.
 507:   - With the choice S² = (log N)^{C₃}/(ρ_rρ_F) of Theorem 4.3: ρ_F/ρ_r = L^{−[h+(d+1)/(4k)]/(2a)+o(1)},
 508:     so S^{−2} ≤ ρ_r² for large N, and this term is absorbed. In general Theorem R needs S² ≥ ρ_r^{−2}.
 509: - **Per pair**, the count is ≤ (M²/ℓ)ρ_r²(log N)^{O(1)}. This bound is independent of q.
 510: - **Counting pairs.** For fixed P, the P′ with ℓ | Δ are the points of Λ_ℓ(g) ∩ 𝒫_ℓ for g = (a, b),
 511:   i.e. g₀/g₁ ≡ a/b. This lattice contains P ∈ 𝒫_ℓ, so it is regular. There are
 512:   ≤ C_abs S² of them (Lemma 1.3). Hence
 513:     R2(ℓ) ≤ #𝒫_ℓ·C_abs S²·|𝒢|^{−1}(M²/ℓ)ρ_r²(log)^{O(1)} ≍ (V²/log V)(S²/ℓ)ρ_r²(log)^{O(1)}
 514:           = S⁴ρ_r²(log)^{O(1)}.
 515: - **Conclusion.** By the lower bound, R1(ℓ) ≥ S²ρ_r/(log)^{O(1)} for every ℓ ∈ J.
 516:   - Across the dyadic interval J, the density arguments X_r, X_F differ by bounded factors. So ρ_r and
 517:     ρ_F, and with them both bounds, are uniform in ℓ up to polylogarithms. Averaging over ℓ then proves
 518:     the theorem.
 519:   - For the filtered count this gives R2,filt(ℓ) ≤ R2(ℓ) ≪ S⁴ρ_r²(log)^{O(1)} and
 520:     R1,filt(ℓ) ≫ S²ρ_r/(log)^{O(1)} directly. Monotonicity of the ratio under filtering is not used. ∎
 521: 
 522: *Remarks.*
 523: 1. **Why it works in 2D and not in 1D.** In (a′_k), the rational pair (s, s′) of a pair moves on the
 524:    *line* b′s − bs′ = Δ/ℓ as m varies (hypD §4.2).
 525:    - Divisor switching then sends the problem to (e, e′) solutions of a single linear equation in a
 526:      box. That count is a Diophantine 0/1 quantity with no area main term, and the method gives
 527:      nothing.
 528:    - Averaging over (b, b′, h) does not repair this: the averaged weight is concentrated on pairs with
 529:      good rational approximations b′/b to s′/s.
 530:    - With (g₀, g₁) random, the pair fills a 2D lattice region of area ≫ perimeter·(lattice scale),
 531:      which is all the argument needs. **This is the "key simplification" the prompt asked about. It is
 532:      not a sieve bound: sieve and Rankin bounds fail at density L^{−c}, see below. It is divisor
 533:      switching, made possible by the 2D randomization.**
 534: 2. **Sieve and Rankin alternatives (prompt item 3, checked).**
 535:    - Shiu and Nair–Tenenbaum upper bounds for Σ F(n)F(n + h) give only ∏_{B<p≤X}(1 − 2/p) ≍
 536:      (log B/log X)², a logarithmic saving against the needed ρ².
 537:    - A Rankin tilt n^{α−1} that recovers the Dickman exponent cannot be localized to an interval or a
 538:      line: extended to all n it is dominated by small n, and a class mod q receives mass ≥ 1 > L(χ₀)/q
 539:      once q > e^{Cu}.
 540:    - Matomäki–Radziwiłł–Tao-type averaged correlations have o(1) or log-power errors, useless at
 541:      density L^{−c}.
 542:    - Pascadi's Cor. 7.2 gives exponent 13/8 − ε < 2. Codex notes that its coefficient condition can
 543:      hold for large fixed k, but the exponent still falls short.
 544:    - So, as far as we can tell, no sieve-type upper bound with loss L^{o(1)} is available in 1D; codex
 545:      reached the same conclusion.
 546: 3. **Precision.** The loss is polylogarithmic, so Theorem R also meets Horn 1's precision requirement
 547:    exp(−o(Λ/k)) for k ≍ (log N)^{1/4}. The remaining Horn-1 obstacles are FM's explicit errors and LD.
 548: 
 549: ## 4. PC_alg: the kernel transfer, the remaining hypothesis, and why known tools fall short
 550: 
 551: ### 4.1 Lemma Kern (the 2D kernel lemma) [proved; tested]
 552: 
 553: **Setup.** Let d ≥ 3, and let P ≠ P′ be primitive with |P|, |P′| ∈ [V/2, √2V] and Δ = det(P, P′) ≠ 0.
 554: Put v = v(P), v′ = v(P′), and Φ: Z^d → Z², c ↦ (⟨c, v⟩, ⟨c, v′⟩). Write P′ ≡ κP (mod Δ); κ exists
 555: because P is primitive.
 556: 
 557: **Statement.**
 558: - (i) Γ := Φ(Z^d) has index |Δ| and equals {(X, X′) : X′ ≡ κ^{d−1}X (mod Δ)}.
 559: - (ii) ker Φ = Q_{PP′}·Z[x, y]_{d−3}, where Q_{PP′} = (bx − ay)(b′x − a′y). This kernel is saturated, and
 560:   its monomial basis has ‖·‖_∞ ≤ 2V² on 𝒫_ℓ, where |a|, |b| ≤ V. In general the bound is
 561:   ≤ 4V² for |P| ≤ √2V, and then H ≥ 2V² is required; only constants change.
 562: - (iii) If H ≥ V², then for every z ∈ Z²,
 563:     Pr_{c∈C_H}[Φ(c) = z] ≤ C_d·|Δ|/(H²‖v ∧ v′‖) ≤ C′_d/(H²V^{2d−4}).
 564: 
 565: *Proof.*
 566: - **(ii).** ⟨c, v(P)⟩ = C(P) for the form C = Σ c_i x^{d−1−i}y^i. C(P) = 0 with P primitive gives
 567:   (bx − ay) | C over Z, by Gauss's lemma. Then C(P′) = 0 and det(P, P′) ≠ 0 give (b′x − a′y) | C/(bx − ay).
 568:   So ker Φ = Q·Z[x, y]_{d−3}. A multiple of Q that is integral is an integral multiple, again by Gauss's
 569:   lemma, so the kernel is saturated.
 570: - **(i).** The index of Φ(Z^d) is the gcd of the 2×2 minors of [v; v′], which is |Δ| (hypD Prop G(i),
 571:   (I6); also codex pcest_1, via a unimodular change sending P to (1, 0)). Since P′ ≡ κP (mod Δ),
 572:   v′ ≡ κ^{d−1}v (mod Δ), so Γ lies in the displayed lattice, which also has index |Δ|. Hence they are
 573:   equal.
 574: - **(iii).**
 575:   - A fibre is (c₀ + K) ∩ C_H, with K = ker Φ of rank d − 2 and det K = ‖v ∧ v′‖/|Δ|. Here we use that
 576:     a saturated lattice and its orthogonal complement in Z^d have the same determinant, and that the row
 577:     lattice of [v; v′] has index |Δ| in its saturation.
 578:   - The translates c + F, where F is the fundamental cell of the basis in (ii), are disjoint. They lie
 579:     in the section of the cube [−(2d−3)H, (2d−3)H]^d by a (d−2)-flat, whose volume is ≤ C_dH^{d−2}.
 580:   - So the fibre has ≤ C_dH^{d−2}|Δ|/‖v ∧ v′‖ points. Divide by (2H + 1)^d.
 581:   - For the second inequality: every 2×2 minor of [v; v′] equals Δ·W_{ij}(P, P′), with W bihomogeneous
 582:     of bidegree (d−2, d−2). For example W_{01} = (aa′)^{d−2}.
 583:   - The vector W(P, P′) of all divided minors never vanishes on (S¹)² (repaired after codex pcest_2).
 584:     - Off the diagonal and antidiagonal, this is injectivity of the projective Veronese map: v(P) ∦ v(P′)
 585:       when P ∦ P′, and Δ ≠ 0.
 586:     - On the diagonal, W(P, P) is the wedge of v(P) with its tangent ∂_u v(P) divided by det(P, u). It
 587:       is nonzero because the rational normal curve is nondegenerate. For example, at P = (1, 0) the
 588:       adjacent divided minor W_{01} equals 1. The antidiagonal is the same up to sign.
 589:     - W is continuous, so by compactness ‖v ∧ v′‖ ≥ c_d|Δ|(|P||P′|)^{d−2}. ∎
 590:     - **Effective version for candidates (Opus proof critic).** The minor (d−2, d−1) equals
 591:       (bb′)^{d−2}Δ. On 𝒫_ℓ we have b, b′ > V/2, so ‖v ∧ v′‖ ≥ |Δ|(V/2)^{2d−4} directly, without any
 592:       compactness argument. This is all that Prop 4.2 uses.
 593:     - The exact identity det K·|Δ| = ‖v ∧ v′‖ and the bound with c_d > 0.05 (d ≤ 7) are checked exactly
 594:       in `tests/test_pcest.py`.
 595: 
 596: *Cost [proved arithmetic].*
 597: - H ≥ V² = ℓS² means h > 1/(2k), which raises the h-floor from LV's 1/(4k) (FM) to 1/(2k).
 598: - Then θ = 2s + (d−1)/(4k) + max(1/(2k), s − (d−2)/(4k)). The floor binds only for k < d(d+1)/4,
 599:   against (d² − 1)/4 before.
 600: - **The infimum over k is unchanged.**
 601: - Finite-k examples: d = 9, k = 20 gives θ = 0.325 instead of 0.3125, so c = 0.937 instead of 0.928. At
 602:   d = 9 the minimal k with c < 1 moves from 11 to 12 (`src/pcest/params.py`). Codex's (5) agrees.
 603: - If one also wanted the moduli G|Δ to stay below |F|^{1/2} (not needed below), the condition would
 604:   become h > s + (6 − d)/(4k) (codex).
