 139: ## 1. Setting
 140: 
 141: - Dixon descent follows `lemmaM` §1–§2.
 142:   - R = r² mod N with r uniform; R is split as u/v (mod N).
 143:   - The large primes ℓ of uv are descended by special-q relations in an LV-randomized field
 144:     A = (m, f = f̂_{N,m} + (x − m)R_A).
 145:   - Theorem DD makes every dependency that contains a descent relation fruitful with probability
 146:     1 − 2^{1−ω}, provided the transcript is invariant under r ↦ εr.
 147:   - All procedures below act on R and A only, so (T) holds.
 148: - L = L_N[1/2, 1]. "L^{o(1)}" means exp(o((log N log log N)^{1/2})), uniformly in N.
 149: - ω = ω(N). Q_N = (Z/N)^{×2}, of index h = 2^ω in (Z/N)^× for odd N.
 150: 
 151: ## 2. Split counting
 152: 
 153: **Lemma Ω [standard].** Put y₀ = exp((log N)^{1/2}(log log N)^{1/4}).
 154: - y₀ is itself L^{o(1)}, so plain trial division to y₀ finds every prime factor ≤ y₀ in L^{o(1)} time.
 155:   The first version used Pollard–Strassen; the critics noted it is unnecessary. Removing small factors
 156:   is routine in rigorous analyses; see e.g. Stange 2022 §4.
 157: - Removing these factors, and prime powers, leaves an N with every prime factor > y₀. Then
 158:   ω ≤ log N/log y₀ = (log N)^{1/2}(log log N)^{−1/4}.
 159: - Hence 2^ω ≤ exp(0.7(log N)^{1/2}(log log N)^{−1/4}) = L^{o(1)}. ∎
 160: 
 161: **Theorem S [proved; a routine adaptation of the Dixon 1981 / Pomerance 1982 random-squares lemma to the ratio form u ≡ Rv].** Let N be odd and Y ≥ 2 with 2Y² < N, and let S ⊂ [Y/2, Y] ∩ Z be any set of
 162: integers coprime to N. Let R be uniform on Q_N, and let E_S be the event that u ≡ Rv (mod N) for some
 163: u, v ∈ S. Then
 164:   Pr[E_S] ≥ |S|⁴ / (2·2^ω·φ(N)·Y²·(1 + log Y)).
 165: Moreover, given R, the event E_S is decided by one Gauss reduction plus at most 2 membership tests per
 166: multiple j·(u₀, v₀), j ≤ Y/max(u₀, v₀). The number of multiples can be ≍ Y in the worst case and is
 167: bounded only in expectation. Theorem K tests only (u₀, v₀), using divisor-closedness.
 168: 
 169: *Proof.*
 170: 1. Put n(R) = #{(u, v) ∈ S² : u ≡ Rv (mod N)}.
 171: 2. **First moment.** Let Ĝ be the group of characters of (Z/N)^× that are trivial on Q_N, so |Ĝ| = h.
 172:    Orthogonality gives 1[x ∈ Q_N] = h^{−1}Σ_{χ∈Ĝ}χ(x). Then
 173:      P := Σ_{R∈Q_N} n(R) = #{(u, v) ∈ S² : uv^{−1} ∈ Q_N} = h^{−1}Σ_χ |Σ_{u∈S}χ(u)|² ≥ |S|²/h.
 174:    Every χ ∈ Ĝ is real, so χ̄(v) = χ(v) and χ(uv^{−1}) = χ(u)χ(v); each summand is a square.
 175: 3. **Second moment.** If (u, v), (u′, v′) ∈ S² both satisfy u ≡ Rv, then uv′ ≡ u′v (mod N). Also
 176:    |uv′ − u′v| < Y² < N, so uv′ = u′v and the two vectors are proportional.
 177:    - Write g = gcd(u, v) and (u₀, v₀) = (u, v)/g. The partners of (u, v) are the vectors j(u₀, v₀) with
 178:      ju₀ ∈ [Y/2, Y].
 179:    - Since u ≥ Y/2, we have u₀ ≥ Y/(2g), so there are at most Y/(2u₀) + 1 ≤ 2g partners.
 180:    - Hence
 181:        Σ_R n(R)² ≤ Σ_{(u,v)∈S², uv⁻¹∈Q_N} 2gcd(u, v) ≤ 2Σ_{g≥1} g·⌊Y/g⌋² ≤ 2Y²(1 + log Y).
 182: 4. **Cauchy–Schwarz.** #{R : n(R) > 0} ≥ P²/Σn(R)². Dividing by |Q_N| = φ(N)/h gives
 183:      Pr[E_S] ≥ (|S|⁴/h²)·h/(φ(N)·2Y²(1 + log Y)).
 184: 5. **Algorithm.** With signed entries, two independent lattice vectors with |u|, |v| ≤ Y have
 185:    0 < |det| ≤ 2Y² < N. But every determinant is a multiple of N, so all such vectors are multiples of the
 186:    primitive shortest vector. The condition 2Y² < N was missing in the first version (critic fix; the
 187:    counting part needs only Y² < N). ∎
 188: 
 189: *Remarks.*
 190: - No independence, no 1/ℓ or saddle-point law, and no character bound is used. The square-class
 191:   condition is absorbed by positivity, which is Dixon's coset pigeonhole Σ_i|S_i|² ≥ |S|²/2^ω written
 192:   with characters.
 193: - The single surviving 2^ω is an artefact of the Cauchy–Schwarz route. Dixon's pointwise multiplicity
 194:   bound loses none.
 195: - The heuristic precedent is the NFS-DL initial split (Joux–Lercier 2003, Commeine–Semaev 2006) and the
 196:   Blake–Fuji-Hara–Mullin–Vanstone u/v trick. We found no rigorous ratio-form statement for composite N
 197:   restricted to square classes; that is a search result.
 198: - The fourth power is the price of the crude multiplicity bound. For smooth-type S one can use
 199:   Ψ(x/d, y) ≪ d^{−α}Ψ(x, y) to recover |S|²·L^{o(1)}. Not needed here.
 200: - E1x (§7) checks the bound exactly in 120 small cases. The true value is about |S|²/φ(N) for random S
 201:   and about 2^ω|S|²/φ(N) when S ⊂ Q_N. So the bound is far from sharp but has the right structure.
 202: 
 203: **Theorem S′ (k-product sets) [label omitted].** Let G be a set of primes in a dyadic interval, coprime to N, with
 204: |G| ≥ 4k². Let S be the products of exactly k distinct primes of G lying in a window [Y/2, Y] (Y² < N),
 205: with |S| ≥ C(|G|, k)/(2k). Then Pr[E_S] ≥ |S|²/(c_k·2^ω·φ(N)) with c_k = 8k²·C(2k, k).
 206: 
 207: *Proof.* As in Theorem S, P ≥ |S|²/h. Split Σ_R n(R)² ≤ Σ_{(u,v)∈S²} #partners(u,v) according to
 208: i = the number of shared primes, so (u, v) = (ju₀, jv₀) with j an i-product.
 209: 1. There are at most C(|G|,i)·C(|G|,k−i)² such pairs.
 210: 2. Each has at most C(|G|,i) partners j′(u₀, v₀).
 211: 3. So the i-th term is ≤ C(|G|,i)²C(|G|,k−i)² ≤ 2C(k,i)²C(|G|,k)² ≤ 8k²C(k,i)²|S|². The middle step uses
 212:    C(n,i)C(n,k−i) = C(k,i)C(n,k)·∏_{t<i}(n−t)/(n−k+i−t) ≤ C(k,i)C(n,k)·e^{k²/(n−k)} ≤ √2·C(k,i)C(n,k) for n ≥ 4k².
 213: 4. Summing, Σ_i C(k,i)² = C(2k,k), so Σn(R)² ≤ c_k|S|². Then use Cauchy–Schwarz as before. ∎
 214: 
 215: This is the |S|² ("λ = 2") form, the Dixon–Pomerance k-product lemma in ratio form. Theorem K(ii)
 216: improves accordingly, to Pr ≥ c′_k·(g_A/(4 log N))^{2k}·2^{−ω} for |G_A| ≥ 4k². E1x (random S) sits at
 217: about 0.9·|S|²/φ(N).
 218: 
 219: ## 3. Theorem K: bounded-k splits reduce Hypothesis D to one averaged statement
 220: 
 221: **Setup.**
 222: - Fix integers k ≥ 1 and odd d ≥ 3. Let X = ⌊√(N/2)⌋.
 223: - Let j be maximal with 2^{kj} ≤ X, and put J = (2^{j−1}, 2^j], so ℓ ∈ J means ℓ = N^{1/(2k)+o(1)}.
 224: - For a field A, let G_A ⊂ J be the set of primes ℓ ∤ N for which a fixed, deterministic,
 225:   capped special-q search succeeds. The search depends only on (A, ℓ), with time cap T_SQ·L^{o(1)}. It
 226:   succeeds when it finds a primitive (a, b) ∈ Λ_ℓ(m) in the box with (a − bm)/ℓ and F_A(a, b) both
 227:   B-smooth.
 228: - Let g_A = |G_A|/π_J with π_J = #(primes in J), and γ := E_A g_A = Pr_{A, ℓ∈J}[ℓ ∈ G_A].
 229: 
 230: **Hypothesis D_k(γ).** γ ≥ L^{−o(1)}, uniformly in N. The search box and the field law are those of §4.
 231: 
 232: **Theorem K [proved, given the setup].**
 233: - (i) Pr_A[g_A ≥ γ/2] ≥ γ/2.
 234: - (ii) Fix a field A with |G_A| ≥ 4k², and let S_A be the products of exactly k distinct primes of
 235:   G_A. The hypothesis on |G_A| was missing; codex fix. Then for N ≥ N₀(k),
 236:     Pr_R[R ≡ u/v with u, v ∈ S_A] ≥ c_k·(g_A/(4 log N))^{4k}·2^{−ω}/(1 + log N), with c_k > 0 depending
 237:     only on k.
 238: - (iii) **[[label omitted]ed after review]**
 239:   - Acceptance rule (codex fix). Accept R when the primitive split (u₀, v₀) has each side a product of
 240:     **at most** k distinct primes of G_A. "Exactly k" is not divisor-closed: cancelling gcd(u, v) can
 241:     remove primes.
 242:   - The count in (ii) still lower-bounds acceptance, because the primitive part of an S_A-pair is
 243:     accepted.
 244:   - In a field with g_A ≥ γ/2 and |G_A| ≥ 4k², a descent attempt succeeds after an expected
 245:     L^{o(1)}·γ^{−2k} R-trials.
 246:   - Each trial must split u₀, v₀ ≈ √N into their prime factors, all of which are ≈ N^{1/(2k)}. This is
 247:     general factoring of √N-size integers, **not** a smoothness test.
 248:   - HECM would cost L_{N^{1/(2k)}}[2/3] = L_N[2/3, c(2k)^{−2/3}]. The first version wrote "L_N[1/3]",
 249:     which is wrong.
 250:   - We use Lenstra–Pomerance's rigorous algorithm instead, at T_shape = L_{√N}[1/2, 1] = L_N[1/2, 1/√2]
 251:     per trial.
 252:   - Each trial with the right shape adds at most 2k special-q searches.
 253: - So the expected cost per descent relation is (T_shape + T_SQ)·L^{o(1)} whenever γ ≥ L^{−o(1)}.
 254: 
 255: *Proof.*
 256: - (i) Since 0 ≤ g_A ≤ 1, we have γ ≤ γ/2 + Pr[g_A ≥ γ/2].
 257: - (ii)
 258:   1. All products of k distinct primes of G_A lie in (2^{k(j−1)}, 2^{kj}] ⊂ (X·2^{−2k}, X]. By the
 259:      pigeonhole principle, one of the 2k dyadic windows [Y/2, Y] with Y ≥ X·2^{−2k} holds at least
 260:      C(|G_A|, k)/(2k) of them.
 261:   2. Distinct prime sets give distinct products, so |S_A ∩ [Y/2, Y]| ≥ (|G_A|/k)^k/(2k).
 262:   3. Use π_J ≥ 2^{j−1}/(2(j−1)log 2) for j ≥ j₀ (Rosser–Schoenfeld) and Y ≤ 2^{kj}. Then
 263:      |S|/Y ≥ (g_A/(4kj log 2))^k/(2k), and 4kj log 2 ≤ 2 log N.
 264:   4. Apply Theorem S with Y² ≥ X²·2^{−4k} ≥ N·2^{−4k−2}.
 265: - (iii) Average over R. The bounded factors (log N)^{O(k)}, 2^{O(k)} and 2^ω are L^{o(1)}, by Lemma Ω.
 266:   The shape test costs T_shape per trial. Conditional on u₀v₀ having the right shape, at most 2k
 267:   special-q searches are run. Pr[shape]/Pr[success] ≤ (log N)^{O(k)}g_A^{−4k}2^{2ω} = L^{o(1)} (upper-bound Pr[shape] by 2·2^ω|S_J|²/φ(N), S_J = all k-products from J), so
 268:   wasted searches cost only L^{o(1)} on average. ∎
 269: 
 270: **Corollary K [proved implication, modulo the LV §6 adaptation that lemmaM Corollary DD also leaves
 271: unwritten; [label omitted]ed after review].** Assume D_k(γ), for k ≥ (3/4)(d+1) (FM's rational-side
 272: range). Then every N is factored in expected time L[1/2, (1+θ(d,k))/√2 + o(1)] for θ < 1, where
 273:   θ(d,k) = 2s + (d−1)/(4k) + max(1/(4k), s − (d−2)/(4k)), s = 1/(d+1).
 274: - The floor 1/(4k) on h comes from LV's kernel mechanism, which needs H ≫ ‖(a,b)‖ (critic fix). It
 275:   matters only for k < (d²−1)/4.
 276: - *Algorithm.*
 277:   1. Field selection (codex repair, replacing an ill-posed pilot and then a guess-and-double that
 278:      could waste a main-scale factor).
 279:      - Sample fresh fields, one capped descent attempt each, until an attempt succeeds. Keep that field.
 280:      - With q_A the per-attempt success probability and μ = E_A q_A, the kept field has law ∝ q_A, so
 281:        E[1/q_A | kept] = Pr(q_A > 0)/μ ≤ 1/μ.
 282:      - Thus C further successes cost C/μ attempts in expectation.
 283:      - By (i) and S′, μ ≥ (γ/2)^{2k+1}L^{−o(1)}.
 284:   2. Collect C·L^{o(1)} pure-descent rows, where C is the column count (factor base plus LV §6
 285:      characters).
 286:   3. Linear algebra, then Theorem DD.
 287: - *Cost.*
 288:   - Take B = L[1/2, a]. Relations cost B·(T_shape + T_SQ) = L[1/2, a + max(1/√2, θ/(2a))], and linear
 289:     algebra costs L[1/2, 2a].
 290:   - For θ < 1 the optimum is at a = θ/√2, giving c = (1+θ)/√2.
 291:   - Without the shape cost, c would be √(2θ). The first version claimed that, but it is not achievable
 292:     with a rigorous shape test at fixed k.
 293:   - Recursive variant: use this algorithm itself as the shape test, assuming D_k at all scales. The
 294:     fixed point c = a + c/√2 with θ/(2a) ≤ c − a gives c = √((1+√2)θ).
 295: - *Sizes.*
 296:   - ‖(a, b)‖ ≤ N^{1/(4k)}S, where S² = T_SQ = L^{O(1)}.
 297:   - The rational cofactor is (a − bm)/ℓ = N^{s − 1/(4k) + o(1)}.
 298:   - F(a, b) = N^{s + h + d/(4k) + o(1)}, where H = N^h is the coefficient height of R_A.
 299:   - The level-½ condition |a − bm| ≤ |F|^{1/2−η} is used by FM (§4). It needs h > s − (d−2)/(4k) + 2η.
 300:   - Adding the rational and algebraic sizes gives θ(d,k) + O(η).
 301: - θ(d,k) ↓ 3s as k → ∞.
 302:   - The displayed limits are infima over growing *fixed* k (codex); they are not attained at any
 303:     finite k. Limits of c = (1+θ)/√2: d = 7: 0.973; d = 9: 0.920 (0.923 at k = 50); d = 11: 0.884; d = 19: 0.814;
 304:     d → ∞: 1/√2.
 305:   - Recursive variant: 0.851 at d = 9.
 306:   - All numbers were checked numerically by us and by both proof critics.
 307: 
 308: **Prop H (the bounded-k dilemma) [proved arithmetic].** With k large primes per split,
 309: Pr[all in G_A] ≈ g_A^{k}.
 310: - **Horn 1: an L^{o(1)} rigorous shape test.** HECM costs L_v[2/3] for primes ≤ v. This is L^{o(1)} iff
 311:   log v = o((log N)^{3/4}(log log N)^{1/4}), which forces k ≥ (log N)^{1/4−o(1)}.
 312:   - Then g_A, and so every averaged estimate, must be accurate to exp(−o((log N)^{1/4}(log log N)^{1/2}))
 313:     (B3).
 314:   - LV-type goodness only gives L^{o(1)} losses in the exponent.
 315:   - lemmaM's B1 = exp((log N)^{3/4}) sits on this horn.
 316: - **Horn 2: bounded k.** g_A = L^{−o(1)} suffices, but every shape test is a factorisation of
 317:   √N-size integers into N^{Θ(1)}-size primes.
 318:   - The best *available* rigorous test is Lenstra–Pomerance, at L_N[1/2, 1/√2].
 319:   - This is an upper bound. No lower bound shows that every rigorous shape test must pay it (codex
 320:     wording fix).
 321: - **Growing k (codex).** Let T = √(log N log log N). Interpolating between the horns requires
 322:   k·log log N = o(T) and k·log(1/γ) = o(T). Writing γ = L^{−o(1)} does not by itself give the second
 323:   condition.
 324: - Either way something is paid. Horn 2 still allows c < 1 conditionally, (1+θ)/√2. But Design V
 325:   (√(2/3)) is better unless d ≥ 19.
 326: 
 327: ## 4. From D_k to a first and a second moment
 328: 
 329: **Field and box law.**
 330: - m is uniform in [N^s/2, N^s].
 331: - f = f̂_{N,m} + (x − m)R_A, where f̂ is LV's non-monic base-m expansion and R_A has degree d − 1 with
 332:   i.i.d. coefficients c uniform in [−H, H], H = N^h.
 333: - ℓ is uniform in the primes of J.
 334: - The box is {(a, b) ∈ Λ_ℓ(m) : |a|, |b| ≤ √ℓ·S}, primitive with b > 0. Irregular lattices (λ₁ ≤ √ℓ/S)
 335:   are excluded; they occur with probability O(S⁻²) over m.
 336: - N_ℓ(A) is the number of box points with (a − bm)/ℓ and F_A(a, b) both B-smooth.
 337: - For primitive (a, b), F_A(a, b) = F̂(a, b) + t·⟨c, v(a, b)⟩, with t = a − bm,
 338:   v(a, b) = (a^{d−1−i}b^i)_{0≤i<d}, and F_A(a, b) ≡ N b^d (mod t).
 339: 
 340: **Prop PZ [label omitted].** γ ≥ (E N)²/E[N²] = 1/(1/E N + PC), where PC := E[N(N−1)]/(E N)² and every E is
 341: over (A, ℓ). So if E N ≥ 1 (hypothesis FM) and PC ≤ L^{o(1)} (hypothesis PC), then D_k(γ) holds with
 342: γ ≥ L^{−o(1)}. ∎
 343: - Here N_ℓ(A) must count the candidates actually covered by the *capped* search that yield *valid
 344:   certificates*. Existence outside the cap does not suffice (codex).
 345: - Irregular lattices, and the finite-interval error from sampling m in an interval rather than uniformly
 346:   mod ℓ, must be built into the moment estimates themselves. An unconditional exclusion probability does
 347:   not replace that. FM's polynomial room M ≫ ℓ makes this repairable (codex).
 348: 
 349: ### 4.1 FM, the first moment [sketch; inputs are proved theorems, but the transfer is not written out]
 350: FM says E N ≥ L^{−o(1)}·S²·ρ(u_rat)·ρ(u_F). Choose S² = T_SQ = L^{o(1)}/(ρ(u_rat)ρ(u_F)).
 351: 1. **Rational side (proved input).**
 352:    - Fix ℓ and primitive (a, b). As m runs over m ≡ a/b (mod ℓ), the cofactor s = (a − bm)/ℓ runs over an
 353:      arithmetic progression of common difference b ≤ √ℓS, with M/ℓ terms of size ≍ bM/ℓ.
 354:    - Harper's BV theorem for smooth numbers applies to these moduli b, which form a *dense* family, so
 355:      the log-power saving suffices. It needs √ℓS ≤ (bM/ℓ)^{1/2−η}, i.e. roughly
 356:      k ≥ (3/4)(d+1)(1+O(η)).
 357: 2. **Algebraic side (LV §5 + LV Lemma 8.5, transplanted).**
 358:    - LV Lemmas 5.13–5.20 show that F_A(a, b) is close to uniform on long progressions mod t.
 359:    - LV's goodness (Def. 5.12) then gives Pr_c[F smooth] ≥ ρ(u_F)^{1+o(1)} for every *good* modulus t.
 360:    - It remains to show that bad moduli are an o(1) proportion of the family 𝒯 = {ℓs : ℓ ∈ J, s B-smooth},
 361:      weighted by the induced law. This is a restricted BV for 𝒯 with relative saving.
 362:    - **Lemma 8.5′ [sketch]** is LV's Lemma 8.5 with y-smooth moduli replaced by 𝒯. Take LV's conductor
 363:      decomposition, with m₀ = min(B^η, e^{η√log x}) and M₀ = x^η, and choose η < 1/(2k(s + h + 1)).
 364:      - Every conductor divisible by ℓ is ≥ ℓ ≥ M₀. Those characters fall in LV's large-conductor range,
 365:        where Harper's Fact 8.11 bounds the sum over *all* moduli by Q√Ψ·log^{7/2}x.
 366:      - Smaller conductors r divide the B-smooth part s. The counts Σ_{q∈𝒯, r|q}1/φ(q) equal LV's sums
 367:        (Claims 8.14, 8.16), multiplied by the common factor Σ_{ℓ∈J}1/ℓ. That factor also multiplies
 368:        |𝒯|, so the relative saving e^{−cu/log²u} + B^{−c} is unchanged.
 369:      - The large-conductor term needs Q ≤ √Ψ(x, B)·δ_𝒯·L^{−ε}, where δ_𝒯 is the density of 𝒯. This holds
 370:        with polynomial room, because t ≤ |F|^{1/2−η}.
 371:    - LV's proof uses one y for both the moduli and the counted numbers; here both are B, plus the prime ℓ.
 372: 3. **Status.** FM is a plausible transplant of LV §5 and §8. It is labelled *sketch*, and the earlier
 373:    "no new idea expected" is withdrawn on codex's points:
 374:    - the smooth variable has length Q/ℓ, so the weights involve densities at Q/(ℓr) and are not LV's
 375:      sums times Σ1/ℓ;
 376:    - the induced multiplicities of t need explicit bounds;
 377:    - Harper's large-conductor term is √Ψ(Q + x^{1/2−η}log²x)log^{7/2}x, not just Q√Ψ.
 378:    Fixed polynomial slack should absorb the last point. One point to check: LV's proof of Lemma 5.21 compares bad
 379:    B-smooth moduli against Ψ(Q_max, B′) rather than Ψ(Q_max, B). This is harmless only when β = β′ (our
 380:    design has a single B), and a referee should confirm it.
 381: 4. **Review additions.**
 382:    - The published smooth-moduli BV results do not cover FM as stated: de la Bretèche–Drappeau JEMS 2020
 383:      Thm 2.1 and Pascadi Thm 7.1. They require P⁺(q₀) ≤ y₂, and their saving carries a factor
 384:      e^{O(u₂)}, which is fatal at y₂ = B = L[1/2].
 385:    - So FM rests on LV's Lemma 8.5 method, whose relative saving ϱ(Q, y)(e^{−cu/log²u} + y^{−c}) has no
 386:      e^{O(u)} loss.
 387:    - The sketch must also pass from "bad t are rare in 𝒯" to "bad t are rare under the induced law of t".
 388:      Here s is B-smooth in a progression mod b, so the bad set could concentrate on progressions.
 389:    - The parameter regime k ≥ (3/4)(d+1), s > 1/(2k) belongs in D_k.
