  80: ## 1. Theorem DD (Dixon descent)
  81: 
  82: **Setting.**
  83: - N is odd with ω ≥ 2 distinct prime factors. Repeated factors are allowed: μ₂(Z/N) ≅ {±1}^ω since each (Z/p^e)^× is
  84:   cyclic. Prime powers are removed first.
  85: - A relation is a pair (X_i, c_i) with X_i ∈ (Z/N)^× and c_i in a certificate group 𝒢, with a homomorphism
  86:   ev: 𝒢 → (Z/N)^× such that X_i² = ev(c_i).
  87:   - Dixon/QS: 𝒢 = Q^× and ev is reduction mod N.
  88:   - NFS: 𝒢 = Q^× × K^× and ev(q, γ) = q·φ(γ)^{−1} on elements coprime to N, with the LV/BLP f′ and f_d factors.
  89: - If ∏_S c_i = g² and g has been computed, put X_S = ∏_S X_i and Y = ev(g). Then X_S² = Y², and Y is a unit.
  90: 
  91: **Descent relations.**
  92: 1. Draw r uniform in (Z/N)^×. A non-unit r is a factor of N: output it if 1 < gcd < N, and resample if gcd = N. The code
  93:    draws r from [2, N−2]. This breaks exactness only on the fibre of R = 1, which has probability 2^ω/N.
  94: 2. Put R = r² mod N.
  95: 3. A procedure 𝒟(R; ξ) runs with fresh coins ξ and run-wide coins Ξ (for example the field (m, f)). Both are independent
  96:    of r. It either fails or returns (W, c) with W²R = ev(c).
  97: 4. Set X = rW.
  98: 
  99: **Theorem DD [label omitted].** Let 𝒯 be the exposed transcript. It contains N; Ξ; all ξ_i, R_i, W_i, c_i; all other relations
 100: (X_j, c_j); the choice of S; and g. Assume:
 101: - (T) 𝒯 is invariant under every replacement r_i ↦ ε_i r_i (ε_i ∈ μ₂), where only the stored roots X_i = r_i W_i transform;
 102: - S contains at least one descent relation.
 103: 
 104: Then Pr[1 < gcd(X_S − Y, N) < N | 𝒯] = 1 − 2^{1−ω} ≥ 1/2.
 105: 
 106: *Proof.*
 107: 1. Conditional on R_i, the root r_i is uniform on r_i·μ₂, and the draws are independent.
 108: 2. By (T), conditioning on 𝒯 leaves the fibre elements i.i.d. uniform on μ₂.
 109: 3. Fix any descent relation i₀ ∈ S. Then X_S/Y = ε_{i₀}·Z with Z 𝒯-measurable given the other ε's, so X_S/Y is uniform
 110:    on μ₂.
 111: 4. Exactly two elements of μ₂ are ±1. For any other ζ, gcd(ζ − 1, N) is the proper divisor ∏_{ζ≡1 (p^e)} p^e. ∎
 112: 
 113: **Remarks.**
 114: - (R1) **(T) is necessary.** For example, a relation with root r^{−1} and certificate R^{−1} makes the product
 115:   identically 1. The selection of S, g or acceptance must not use X_i, Jacobi symbols of X_i, or earlier gcd outcomes.
 116:   The statement is per dependency: after an observed trivial gcd, take fresh hidden roots or non-adaptive kernel samples.
 117: - (R2) **No leak.** The split, the HECM or trial-division tests, the special-q searches, and LV's character and
 118:   square-root steps act on R, u, v, ℓ, (a, b) and the field only. A factorisation of an integer computed from R is a
 119:   function of R.
 120: - (R3) **Consistency with the earlier barriers.** The rejected strategies needed the uniform square itself to be
 121:   factor-base-smooth (nfsfruit_1 §3.1; lemmaM_1 (22): cost L[2/3]), or a uniform λ = r² with small ratio representatives
 122:   (nfsfruit Prop B; lemmaM_1 (24)). Descent needs neither. "Re-randomising a known root is factoring" is respected: descent
 123:   never sees a root.
 124: - (R4) **Prior art.**
 125:   - Rabin 1979 (square roots and factoring); Dixon 1981 and Pomerance's random-squares analyses (fibre symmetry);
 126:     nfsfruit Theorem L1 (the same exact statement for one shared hidden root).
 127:   - Stange 2022: random exponents in index calculus mod n, with order finding and rational linear algebra under her
 128:     Hypothesis 3.1. Her NFS version is heuristic.
 129:   - NFS-DL individual-log descent.
 130:   - DD only packages these for an NFS factor base.
 131: 
 132: ## 2. Cost of Dixon descent (heuristic smoothness model; implications from Hypothesis D)
 133: 
 134: **One-level descent.**
 135: - *Split.* (u, v) is the Gauss-reduced shortest vector of {u ≡ Rv (mod N)}, so |u|, |v| ≤ √(2N). Accept iff u, v are
 136:   B1-smooth.
 137: - *Special-q.* For each odd-valuation prime ℓ ∈ (B, B1] of uv, search the special-q box of Λ_ℓ = {a ≡ bm (mod ℓ)}. This
 138:   needs a regular reduced basis ≍ √ℓ; for random m the irregular case, m/ℓ close to a low-height rational, is rare. The
 139:   search looks for primitive (a, b) with (a − bm)/ℓ and F(a, b) both B-smooth.
 140: - *Substitution.* Then ℓ ≡ φ(a − bα)/c with c = (a − bm)/ℓ.
 141: - *Rows and cost.* Use C·L^{o(1)} pure-descent rows; LV §6 needs extra character columns and combinations. Every nonzero
 142:   dependency then contains a descent relation. Total cost ≈ C·L^{o(1)}·(k·T_SQ + T_split) + T_LA.
 143: 
 144: **(a) Fixed odd d, L[1/2] scale.**
 145: - Use a non-monic f of degree d with m = N^{s+o(1)}, ‖f‖ = N^{s+o(1)}, s = 1/(d+1). Take B = L[1/2, a] and
 146:   B1 = exp((log N)^{3/4}).
 147: - Rigorous HECM (Lenstra–Pila–Pomerance) tests B1-smoothness in L_{B1}[2/3] = exp(O((log N)^{1/2}(log log N)^{1/3})) =
 148:   L[1/2, o(1)].
 149: - The split succeeds with probability ρ(u₁)² = L^{−o(1)}, u₁ = (log N)^{1/4}/2. Each split has k ≍ (log N)^{1/4} + O(log log N)
 150:   large primes.
 151: - The ℓ^{(d−1)/2} and box factors are N^{o(1)}. So both sides have size N^{θ+o(1)}, θ = 2s, and T_SQ = L[1/2, θ/(2a)].
 152: - Optimum a = 1/√(d+1), giving **L[1/2, 2/√(d+1)]**: d = 3: 1; d = 5: 0.816; d = 7: 0.707. This is the formal constant of
 153:   plain fixed-degree NFS.
 154: - **(a′) Level-of-distribution-compatible design.**
 155:   - With LV randomization, F(a, b) ≡ N b^d (mod t) with t = a − bm. At the sizes above, t = |F|^{1−o(1)} (codex lemmaM_2),
 156:     which no BV-type theorem for smooth numbers reaches.
 157:   - Inflate the coefficients of R to H ≥ M^{2+ε}. Then t ≤ |F|^{1/2−ε}, the range of LV's own §8 analysis.
 158:   - This costs F ≍ N^{2s} and the rational cofactor ≍ N^{s}, so θ = 3/(d+1) and c = √(6/(d+1)): < 1 for odd d ≥ 7.
 159:   - The moduli are still multiples of ℓ with a moving residue N b^d. Their first moment is part of Hypothesis D and is open.
 160: 
 161: **(b) One level, growing degree.**
 162: - B = L[α], log B1 ≍ (log N)^{β₁}(log log N)^{1−β₁}, d ≍ (log N/log log N)^{(1−β₁)/2}.
 163: - The split forces β₁ ≥ 1 − α. The special-q values have log-size ≍ (log N)^{(1+β₁)/2}(log log N)^{(1−β₁)/2}, which forces
 164:   β₁ ≤ 4α − 1. Hence α ≥ 2/5, with equality at β₁ = 3/5, d ≍ (log N/log log N)^{1/5}, and log-sizes
 165:   ≍ (log N)^{4/5}(log log N)^{1/5}.
 166: - HECM on B1 = L[3/5] costs L_{B1}[2/3] = L[2/5].
 167: - So L[2/5, c] for an unoptimised c. The one-stage 2/5 is in Gordon's NFS-DL analysis (1993). Only the factoring and
 168:   fruitfulness packaging is ours.
 169: - With k ≍ (log N/log log N)^{2/5}, a per-target success probability (log N)^{−O(1)} still gives L[2/5, c′].
 170: 
 171: **(c) No rigorous L[1/3] from this architecture.**
 172: - Rigorous HECM at B1 = L[2/3] costs L[4/9]. More generally, HECM splitting needs 2β₁/3 ≤ α, and with 1 − β₁ ≤ α this
 173:   again forces α ≥ 2/5 (codex lemmaM_2).
 174: - One-level descent of L[2/3] primes costs L[1/2] at the re-optimised degree d ≍ (log N)^{1/6}, or L[2/3] at the L[1/3]-NFS
 175:   degree.
 176: - Multi-level descent would also make the special-q primes field-dependent. Calling this "strictly harder" is an
 177:   assessment.
 178: 
 179: **(d) QS analogue (the toy of E1).**
 180: - Special-q relation x² − N = ℓ·c with x ≡ ±√N (mod ℓ), scanned near √N. The cofactor ≍ 2√N·J is independent of ℓ.
 181: - Splits are rejected if a large prime is inert in Q(√N). Example: N = 77, R = 16 ≡ 3/5, and both 3 and 5 are inert. The
 182:   rejection costs 2^{−k} = L^{−o(1)}.
 183: - Heuristic L[1/2, 1], with fruitfulness from DD.
 184: 

