 310:     54–62) and Kaltofen–Saunders (AAECC-9, LNCS 539 (1991), Thms 2–4; Thm 4 includes uniform sampling of the
 311:     solution manifold). KS's rows²·polylog bound needs the Toeplitz preconditioner products done with fast
 312:     polynomial multiplication. LP 1992 §10 (Alg. 10.1, Thm 10.3) uses Wiedemann's coordinate recurrence to find
 313:     one dependency; it did not need uniform sampling.
 314:   - "x − A⁻(Ax)" gives a uniform element of the kernel, given the solver's coins. Coordinate expansion over an F₂
 315:     basis of F_{2^e} returns an F₂ kernel vector.
 316: - Each kernel vector is verified, so the method is Las Vegas.
 317: - Expected cost O(M_rows·w + M_rows²)·(log N)^{O(1)} = (M_rows)^{2}(log N)^{O(1)}.
 318: - dim ker =: r ≥ 12. Two independent uniform samples are linearly independent with probability
 319:   (1 − 2^{−r})(1 − 2^{1−r}) ≥ 1 − 3·2^{−12} (codex lv6_2 [label omitted]ed our 2^{−11}). The third test vector s₁ + s₂
 320:   costs nothing.
 321: - **Uniform binary sampling, precisely.** Draw x uniform in F₂^{M_rows}. Solve Aᵀy = Aᵀx over F_{2^e}; the solver sees
 322:   only Aᵀx and its own coins. Return one **fixed** F₂-coordinate (in a fixed F₂-basis of F_{2^e} containing 1) of
 323:   x − y, and verify it. Conditional on Aᵀx, x is uniform on a coset of the kernel, so the output is uniform on the
 324:   F₂-kernel. Pad Aᵀ to a square matrix for the Kaltofen–Saunders preconditioner.
 325: - **Why three tests suffice [label omitted].** φ(s₁), φ(s₂) ∈ H₀, and |H₀| ≤ 2. Either one of them is 0, or both equal the
 326:   unique nonzero element, and then φ(s₁ + s₂) = 0. In every case some S ∈ {s₁, s₂, s₁ + s₂} has γ′_S ∈ K^{×2},
 327:   and S ≠ ∅.
 328: 
 329: ### 3.5 Square root in K: Algorithm SQ and Lemma SQ
 330: 
 331: **Algorithm SQ.**
 332: - **Input.**
 333:   - Γ ∈ Z[β], given as g′(β)² times a product of atoms z_P (with multiplicities).
 334:   - An integer A with this property: if Γ = η² for some η ∈ Z[β], then ‖η‖_∞ ≤ A.
 335: - **Steps.**
 336:   1. Let p be the least prime in (B, 2B] with p ∤ D. It exists because (B, 2B] has ≫ B/log B primes and
 337:      ω(D) = O(log N). It is found by sieving (B, 2B] once per field, in Õ(B) = L^{a+o(1)} time. (Alternatively reuse
 338:      a character prime r_j > Δ̄.) Then g mod p is squarefree, and Γ is a unit at p, because Norm(Γ) = ±disc(g)²·∏(t^{d−1}F(a_P, b_P))^{|e_P|}
 339:      has only prime factors ≤ B or dividing D.
 340:   2. Factor ḡ = φ₁⋯φ_τ over F_p (τ ≤ d), Las Vegas. In each F_p[y]/φ_j, test whether Γ is a square (Euler) and take
 341:      a square root ρ_j (Tonelli–Shanks in F_{p^{deg φ_j}}, Las Vegas). If some component is a non-square, output
 342:      NONSQUARE.
 343:   3. For each of the 2^{τ−1} sign vectors ε with ε₁ = +1:
 344:      - CRT the values ε_jρ_j to ρ_ε ∈ Z[β]/p.
 345:      - Starting from z₀ := ρ_ε^{−1}, Newton-lift the inverse square root, z ↦ z + z(1 − Γz²)/2 mod p^{2^i}, to the
 346:        first precision p^e > 2A.
 347:      - Put η_ε := Γz mod p^e with centred coefficients. If η_ε² = Γ exactly, output η_ε.
 348:   4. Output NONSQUARE.
 349: 
 350: **Lemma SQ [label omitted].**
 351: - (i) **[label omitted]ness.**
 352:   - Any output η satisfies η² = Γ exactly.
 353:   - If Γ ∈ K^{×2}, then Γ = η² with η ∈ Z[β], and SQ outputs ±η.
 354: - (ii) **The bound A.** Let Mh := ⌊‖g‖₂⌋ + 1 (≥ Mahler(g) ≥ max_k max(1, |β_k|)) and g_d := d‖g‖₁Mh^{d−1}. Then
 355:   A := d·⌈U^{1/2}⌉·2^{d−1}Mh·g_d^{d−1}, U := g_d²·∏_P (‖z_P‖₁Mh^{d−1})^{|e_P|},
 356:   is valid. log₂A ≤ ½·Σ_P|e_P|·log₂(‖z_P‖₁Mh^{d−1}) + O(d² log(d‖g‖₁Mh)) = O_d((1 + Σ|e_P|)·log N).
 357:   (The numerator estimate uses the product defining the Mahler measure:
 358:   |[y^j]∏_{ℓ≠k}(y − β_ℓ)| ≤ C(d−1, j)·Mahler(g) ≤ 2^{d−1}Mh.)
 359: - (iii) **Cost.** The cost is O_d(𝖬(log A)·log log A) + (log N)^{O(1)} bit operations, where 𝖬 is integer
 360:   multiplication time. This is quasi-linear in the size of Γ.
 361: 
 362: *Proof.*
 363: - **(i).**
 364:   - Write Γ = g′(β)²·γ with γ := ∏ z_P^{|e_P|} ∈ Z[β].
 365:   - If Γ = δ², δ ∈ K, then (δ/g′(β))² = γ ∈ O_K, so δ/g′(β) ∈ O_K.
 366:   - g′(β)O_K ⊂ Z[β]: indeed O_K ⊂ O_K^∨ ⊂ Z[β]^∨ = g′(β)^{−1}Z[β] (Euler's lemma; Z[β] ⊂ O_K). Hence
 367:     η := δ ∈ Z[β].
 368:   - p ∤ disc(g), so Z_p[β] ≅ ∏_j Z_p[y]/(G_j) is a product of unramified local rings, with G_j the Hensel lifts of
 369:     the φ_j.
 370:   - η mod p equals ±ρ_ε for exactly one class ε with ε₁ = +1.
 371:   - For p odd and Γ a unit, a unit square root with prescribed residue lifts uniquely (Hensel). So
 372:     η_ε ≡ ±η (mod p^e). Since p^e > 2A ≥ 2‖η‖_∞, centring recovers ±η exactly.
 373:   - Conversely, the check η_ε² = Γ is exact. NONSQUARE at step 2 is [label omitted] because η ∈ O_K would reduce to a
 374:     square in O_K/𝔓 = F_p[y]/φ_j (p is prime to the index).
 375: - **(ii).**
 376:   - |σ_k(z)| ≤ ‖z‖₁Mh^{d−1} and |g′(β_k)| ≤ g_d, so |σ_k(η)|² ≤ U.
 377:   - Coefficients come from the inverse Vandermonde: (V^{−1})_{jk} = [y^j](g(y)/(y − β_k))/g′(β_k).
 378:     - The numerator coefficients are ≤ 2^{d−1}Mh.
 379:     - ∏_k |g′(β_k)| = |disc g| ≥ 1 gives |g′(β_k)| ≥ g_d^{−(d−1)}.
 380: - **(iii).**
 381:   - Γ is computed by a product tree.
 382:   - Newton doubles the precision. Each step is O(d²) multiplications of ≤ log₂(p^e) = O(log A) bits.
 383:   - The sign enumeration multiplies by 2^{d−1} = O(1). ∎
 384: - (E1: all 92 NONSQUARE outputs were independently certified, and all 116 squares were verified. The ratio
 385:   log₂‖η‖/log₂A was 0.08–0.22, so A is conservative but polynomially tight.)
 386: - **Prior art (literature critic).** Algorithm SQ is the p-adic method of BLP §9 (LNM 1554, pp. 71–75).
 387:   - BLP already Newton-iterate the inverse square root to twice a proved coefficient bound.
 388:   - They optionally verify exactly ("free ourselves from … the unproved conjecture of Section 8").
 389:   - At a squarefree, non-inert prime they use the 2^t − 1 starting values, after Weinberger–Rothschild (ACM TOMS 2
 390:     (1976)).
 391:   - What is ours: the explicit bound A for the non-monic Z[β], the Hensel-prime choice and the cost at our heights.
 392: - Couveignes (LNM 1554) needs odd degree and inert primes. Montgomery's and Nguyen's methods are lattice-based
 393:   and heuristic.
 394: - LV cite Montgomery "in time O(M²)" without a rigorous analysis. Lemma SQ, that is BLP §9 made explicit,
 395:   replaces that step.
 396: 
 397: ### 3.6 The congruence and ψ
 398: 
 399: - Let S be the chosen dependency, e_P := e_P(S), E := E(S) (even), and γ⁻ := ∏_{e_P<0} z_P^{|e_P|}.
 400: - ψ is a ring homomorphism on Z[β], so ψ(η)² = ψ(Γ_S) = ψ(g′(β))²·∏_P ψ(z_P)^{|e_P|}.
 401: - Hence, with Y as in step 5,
 402:   Y² = q_S·t^{−E}·∏_P ψ(z_P)^{|e_P|}/ψ(γ⁻)² = q_S·t^{−E}·∏_P ψ(z_P)^{e_P} ≡ ∏_{i∈S} r_i² (mod N) by (1.1).
 403: - **Units.**
 404:   - ψ(z_P) = tG(P)/g₁ is a unit: G(P) = σℓn, with n B-smooth, and ℓ ∤ N, otherwise a factor was found.
 405:   - ψ(g′(β)) = t^{d−2}f′(g₀/g₁) is a unit by the field check (§4).
 406:   - √q_S involves only g₁ and primes ≤ B.
 407: - No map on K^× is needed. Every identity is a ring identity in Z[β] or in Q, followed by reduction mod N.
 408: - E1 checks this exactly: 0 violations in 116 cases.
 409: - **Final rational square root.** √q_S is computed from the recorded exponent vectors (halving), with
 410:   M_rows·k·(log N)^{O(1)} operations. No square root mod N is ever extracted.
 411: 
 412: ### 3.7 Transcript invariance (T)
 413: 
 414: Theorem DD needs the exposed transcript 𝒯 to be invariant under r_i ↦ ε_ir_i. Every quantity computed by LV6 is
 415: a function of (field, R_i, splits, atoms, certificates) and fresh coins:
 416: - the columns, the characters (r_j, s_j), the kernel vectors, the Hensel prime p, the branch ε, η, the choice
 417:   among s₁, s₂, s₁ + s₂, and Y.
 418: 
 419: Field rejections (§4) depend on the field only. None of these reads r_i, a Jacobi symbol of r_i, or the outcome of
 420: an earlier final gcd(X_S − Y, N) involving the hidden roots. (Public gcd checks on field and certificate data are
 421: invariant and allowed.) So (T) holds, and DD gives Pr[nontrivial | 𝒯] = 1 − 2^{1−ω}.
 422: - **Retry policy.**
 423:   - After a trivial gcd we restart with fresh rows, as in lemmaM's remark R1.
 424:   - (Optional refinement.) Reusing the rows with a new dependency S′ is also exact, provided S′ ∉ {0, S}:
 425:     conditioned on the outcome for S, ∏_{S′}ε_i is still uniform on μ₂. But a policy that adapts the *choice* of
 426:     S′ must keep S′ outside the span of all previously tested S. We do not need this.
 427: 
 428: ### 3.8 Cost and the five requirements (a)–(e)
 429: 
 430: | item | where | cost / status |
 431: |---|---|---|
 432: | (a) heights V = N^{1/(4k)}L^{O(1)} | only in log A = O(k·M_rows·log N) | Lemma SQ quasi-linear; no box enumeration in post-processing |
 433: | (b) non-monic (f, g₁x − g₀) | β = f_dα, monic g, ψ(β) = f_d g₀/g₁; bad primes in H | Lemmas C, H, SQ |
 434: | (c) the g₁/f_d parity column | one column E_i mod 2 | §3.1 |
 435: | (d) (rows)^{2+o(1)} | Wiedemann L^{2a+o(1)}; characters, SQ (3 calls), √q_S at L^{a+o(1)} | §3.3–§3.6 |
 436: | (e) transcript invariance (T) | all steps are functions of certificates and coins | §3.7 |
